1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,091
Good morning everyone,

2
00:00:02,091 --> 00:00:05,470
and welcome to the Museum of Farming Life.

3
00:00:05,470 --> 00:00:08,901
I understand it’s your first visit here,

4
00:00:08,901 --> 00:00:13,758
so I’d like to give you some background information about the museum

5
00:00:13,758 --> 00:00:17,450
and then explain a little about what you can see during your visit.

6
00:00:17,450 --> 00:00:23,742
So, where we’re standing at the moment is the entrance to a large building

7
00:00:23,742 --> 00:00:30,976
that was constructed in 1880 as the home of a local businessman, Alfred Palmer,

8
00:00:30,976 --> 00:00:32,780
of the Palmer biscuit factory.

9
00:00:32,780 --> 00:00:38,934
It was later sold and became a hall of residence for students in 1911,

10
00:00:38,934 --> 00:00:41,199
and a museum in 1951.

11
00:00:41,199 --> 00:00:50,780
In 2005, a modern extension was built to accommodate the museum’s collections.

12
00:00:50,780 --> 00:00:53,986
The museum’s owned by the university,

13
00:00:53,986 --> 00:01:00,398
and apart from two rooms that are our offices, the university uses the main

14
00:01:00,398 --> 00:01:02,230
part of the building.

15
00:01:02,230 --> 00:01:05,678
You may see students going into the building for lessons,

16
00:01:05,678 --> 00:01:09,470
but it’s not open to museum visitors, I’m afraid.

17
00:01:09,470 --> 00:01:16,408
It’s a shame because the interior architectural features are outstanding, especially the room that

18
00:01:16,408 --> 00:01:18,720
used to be the library.

19
00:01:18,720 --> 00:01:24,300
Luckily, we’ve managed to keep entry to the museum free.

20
00:01:24,300 --> 00:01:32,084
This includes access to all the galleries, outdoor areas and the rooms for special

21
00:01:32,084 --> 00:01:32,640
exhibitions.

22
00:01:32,640 --> 00:01:39,041
We run activities for children and students, such as the museum club, for which

23
00:01:39,041 --> 00:01:40,870
there’s no charge.

24
00:01:40,870 --> 00:01:47,561
We do have a donation box just over there so feel free to give

25
00:01:47,561 --> 00:01:49,950
whatever amount you consider appropriate.

26
00:01:49,950 --> 00:01:54,890
We do have a cloakroom, if you’d like to leave your coats

27
00:01:54,890 --> 00:01:56,030
and bags somewhere.

28
00:01:56,030 --> 00:01:59,514
Unlike other museums, photography is allowed here,

29
00:01:59,514 --> 00:02:04,490
so you might like to keep your cameras with you.

30
00:02:04,490 --> 00:02:10,142
You might be more comfortable not carrying around heavy rucksacks, though keep your coats

31
00:02:10,142 --> 00:02:15,390
and jackets on as it’s quite cold in the museum garden today.

32
00:02:15,390 --> 00:02:17,850
I’d like to tell you about the different areas of the museum.

33
00:02:17,850 --> 00:02:19,184
Just inside,

34
00:02:19,184 --> 00:02:27,190
and outside the main gallery, we have an area called Four Seasons.

35
00:02:27,190 --> 00:02:31,460
Here you can watch a four-minute animation of a woodland scene.

36
00:02:31,460 --> 00:02:36,895
It was designed especially for the museum by a group of young people on

37
00:02:36,895 --> 00:02:38,447
a film studies course,

38
00:02:38,447 --> 00:02:40,000
and it’s beautiful.

39
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:41,822
Children absolutely love it,

40
00:02:41,822 --> 00:02:42,733
but then,

41
00:02:42,733 --> 00:02:44,100
so do adults.

42
00:02:44,100 --> 00:02:48,100
The main gallery’s called Town and Country.

43
00:02:48,100 --> 00:02:54,268
It includes a photographic collection of prizewinning sheep and shepherds.

44
00:02:54,268 --> 00:02:59,203
Leaving Town and Country, you enter Farmhouse Kitchen,

45
00:02:59,203 --> 00:03:01,670
which is … well, self-explanatory.

46
00:03:01,670 --> 00:03:06,139
Here we have the oldest collection of equipment for making butter

47
00:03:06,139 --> 00:03:08,170
and cheese in the country.

48
00:03:08,170 --> 00:03:14,153
And this morning, a specialist cheesemaker will be giving demonstrations of how it’s

49
00:03:14,153 --> 00:03:14,580
produced.

50
00:03:14,580 --> 00:03:17,040
You may even get to try some.

51
00:03:17,040 --> 00:03:21,750
After that, you can go in two directions.

52
00:03:21,750 --> 00:03:26,510
To the right is a staircase that takes you up to a landing from

53
00:03:26,510 --> 00:03:29,230
where you can look down on the galleries.

54
00:03:29,230 --> 00:03:33,900
To the left is a room called A Year on the Farm.

55
00:03:33,900 --> 00:03:38,267
There’s lots of seating here as sometimes we use the room for school

56
00:03:38,267 --> 00:03:38,579
visits,

57
00:03:38,579 --> 00:03:42,010
so it’s a good place to stop for a rest.

58
00:03:42,010 --> 00:03:47,591
If you’re feeling competitive, you can take our memory test in which you answer

59
00:03:47,591 --> 00:03:50,940
questions about things you’ve seen in the museum.

60
00:03:50,940 --> 00:03:54,378
The next area’s called Wagon Walk.

61
00:03:54,378 --> 00:03:59,780
This contains farm carts from nearly every part of the country.

62
00:03:59,780 --> 00:04:04,260
It’s surprising how much regional variation there was.

63
00:04:04,260 --> 00:04:09,230
Beside the carts are display boards with information about each one.

64
00:04:09,230 --> 00:04:11,515
The carts are old and fragile,

65
00:04:11,515 --> 00:04:15,703
so we ask you to keep your children close to you

66
00:04:15,703 --> 00:04:19,130
and ensure they don’t climb on the carts.

67
00:04:19,130 --> 00:04:23,777
From Wagon Walk, you can either make your way back to reception

68
00:04:23,777 --> 00:04:28,811
or go out into the garden – or even go back to take another

69
00:04:28,811 --> 00:04:30,360
look in the galleries.

70
00:04:30,360 --> 00:04:34,185
In the far corner of the garden is Bees are Magic,

71
00:04:34,185 --> 00:04:39,052
but we’re redeveloping this area so you can’t visit that at the

72
00:04:39,052 --> 00:04:39,400
moment.

73
00:04:39,400 --> 00:04:42,650
You can still buy our honey in the shop, though.

74
00:04:42,650 --> 00:04:45,388
Finally, there’s The Pond,

75
00:04:45,388 --> 00:04:49,220
which contains all kinds of interesting wildlife.

76
00:04:49,220 --> 00:04:54,129
There are baby ducks that are only a few days old, as well as

77
00:04:54,129 --> 00:04:54,830
tiny frogs.

78
00:04:54,830 --> 00:04:59,504
The Pond isn’t deep and there’s a fence around it,

79
00:04:59,504 --> 00:05:02,230
so it’s perfectly safe for children.
